From e7b866fbe3ea4bca908c87f55169fd5b8236aba0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jacob Root <me@rooty.dev>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 06:09:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] vm_control: hold virtio-nvgpu's memory tubes, and map UVM
 pools

virtio-nvgpu's memory requests reach the main process over its two
VmMemoryRequest tubes. Nothing the device process says is taken on trust:
sys::linux::nvgpu holds each tube to what the device needs, against the BAR
layout the PCI transport reports from the main process before the device
process is made (SharedMemoryBarLayout).

The shared memory tube may prepare region 1 (the window) -- only the window,
not the whole BAR -- map descriptors into it and unmap only what it mapped.
A window mapping must be a descriptor, at its own allocation, page-aligned,
inside the window, clear of its other mappings, coherent, at most 16384 at
once, from an NVIDIA device (major 195) or a DRM node (226) -- the only files
the backend places there -- and writable only if opened read-write. Anything
else a VmMemoryRequest can ask -- another allocation, a guest physical
address, a non-descriptor source, ballooning, ioevents -- is refused. The
ioevent tube may register ioevents and nothing else.

Two new requests place CUDA's UVM semaphore pools in region 2, the UVM
aperture: RegisterUvmPool maps a UVM file at the host address that is its
file offset (UVM takes no other), checks the pages are present with mincore,
and gives it a KVM memory slot at an aperture offset; UnregisterUvmPool takes
the slot away, then the mapping. The main process checks the address is
page-aligned and in [4 GiB, 32 TiB), the length non-zero, whole pages and at
most 64 MiB, the offset inside the aperture it laid out and clear of live
pools, the file offset equal to the address, the descriptor really
/dev/nvidia-uvm (minor 0 of the major /proc/devices gives, or its
/proc/self/fd link) opened read-write, and at most 64 pools and 256 MiB per
device. Withdrawal must name a live pool exactly. When the tube goes away,
every pool and window mapping it made is withdrawn. Refusals are logged,
the first eight and then every 1024th. Unrestricted tubes refuse both.

reserve_uvm_band reserves [4 GiB, 32 TiB) PROT_NONE, MAP_NORESERVE, before
anything else can be placed there. A pool is then mapped with MAP_FIXED over
that reservation only (a range the band tracks: never a live pool, never a
range left unknown by a failure), and withdrawal puts the reservation back
in the same step. A failed placement refills any hole it left (checked
against /proc/self/maps) or never maps there again. UVM does not take a
mapping moved in with mremap (its offset must equal the address at mmap
time, and a semaphore pool's vm_open disables a moved vma), so the window's
map-then-move placement cannot be used; without the band a pool is mapped
with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE.

